var e={"ph-meditations-6-48":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`When you want to lift your spirits, think of the good qualities of the people you live among: one person's energy, another's modesty, another's generosity. Nothing cheers the heart like the virtues that show in the character of those around us.`]},"ph-analects-4-21":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "You must always know your parents' ages: on the one hand it brings you joy that they have lived long, and on the other it makes you careful of them as they grow older."`]},"ph-epicurus-55":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`We should soothe our misfortunes by remembering with gratitude the good things we have lost, and by accepting that what has happened cannot be undone.`]},"ph-seneca-81":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Nothing is more honorable than a grateful heart. Those who remember a kindness are already made richer in spirit by the remembering alone.`]},"ph-amicitia-20":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Apart from wisdom, I think nothing better than friendship has been given to human beings.`]},"ph-analects-2-17":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "You, shall I teach you what knowing is? To say you know when you know, and to say you do not know when you do not know: that is knowing."`]},"ph-analects-7-22":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "When three of us walk together, there is surely a teacher for me among them. I choose what is good in them and follow it, and what is not good I use to correct myself."`]},"ph-daodejing-22":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Not showing themselves, they are clearly seen; not claiming to be right, they shine; not boasting, they have merit; not exalting themselves, they endure. Because they do not contend, no one in the world can contend with them.`]},"ph-enchiridion-46":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Never call yourself a philosopher, and do not lecture people on principles. Act on them instead. Sheep do not bring up their grass to show the shepherd how much they ate; they digest it inside and give wool and milk.`]},"ph-mencius-3-8":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius said, "When others pointed out his faults, Zilu was glad. When Yu heard good words, he bowed. Great Shun went further still: he did good together with others, setting himself aside to follow them, and delighted in taking what was good from others to do good."`]},"ph-analects-9-28":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Only when the year turns cold do we see that the pine and the cypress are the last to fade."`]},"ph-analects-2-24":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`To see what is right and not do it is a lack of courage.`]},"ph-mencius-12-15":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`When Heaven is about to give someone a great task, it first troubles their mind, tires their body, and lets their efforts go awry. This is to stir their heart, strengthen their patience, and enable them to do what they could not do before.`]},"ph-meditations-5-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`At dawn, when you are reluctant to get up, think: "I am rising to do the work of a human being." Why complain about going to do what I was born for? Was I made to lie warm under the blankets?`]},"ph-seneca-13":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`There are more things that frighten us than things that crush us, and we suffer more often in imagination than in reality. Do not make yourself unhappy ahead of time over what has not yet come.`]},"ph-analects-15-24":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Zigong asked, "Is there one word that one can practice for a whole lifetime?" The Master said, "It is perhaps 'reciprocity': what you do not wish for yourself, do not do to others."`]},"ph-meditations-6-6":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The best way to answer someone who has wronged you is not to become like them.`]},"ph-meditations-2-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Say to yourself each morning: today I will meet people who are meddling, ungrateful, arrogant, envious. They are like this because they do not know what is good and what is bad. But I cannot be harmed by them, nor can I be angry with those who share my nature, for we were born to work together.`]},"ph-analects-15-15":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Hold yourself strictly to account and others lightly, and resentment will stay far away."`]},"ph-meditations-7-22":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`It is a mark of humanity to love even those who stumble. You can do this when you remember that they are your kin, that they go wrong out of ignorance and without meaning to, and above all that they have not harmed your inner self at all.`]},"ph-analects-1-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Is it not a joy to learn and, in due time, to practice what you have learned? Is it not a delight when a friend comes from far away? Is it not the mark of a noble person to feel no resentment when others do not recognize you?"`]},"ph-daodejing-33":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Those who know others are wise; those who know themselves are enlightened. Those who overcome others have force; those who overcome themselves are strong. Those who know what is enough are rich.`]},"ph-enchiridion-5":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`What troubles people is not things themselves but their judgments about things. So when we are troubled or upset, let us not blame others, but look at ourselves, that is, at our own judgments.`]},"ph-analects-2-15":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "To learn without thinking leaves you in the dark; to think without learning leaves you in danger."`]},"ph-pensees-347":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`A human being is only a reed, the weakest thing in nature; but it is a thinking reed.`]},"ph-daodejing-81":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The sage does not hoard. Having done everything for others, the sage has more; having given everything to others, the sage has even more.`]},"ph-mencius-1-7":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Extend the respect you show the elders of your own family to the elders of other families, and the care you show your own children to the children of others, and the world can be turned in the palm of your hand.`]},"ph-seneca-6":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`I am glad to learn something chiefly because I can teach it. No good thing is pleasant to possess without a friend to share it.`]},"ph-analects-6-30":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`A person of humaneness, wishing to stand firm, helps others stand firm, and wishing to succeed, helps others succeed. To be able to take what is near, oneself, as the measure for understanding others: this may be called the way of humaneness.`]},"ph-meditations-5-6":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Some people, when they do a kindness, expect to be repaid. Another is like a vine: having borne its grapes, it asks for nothing more, and bears grapes again in the next season. So a person who has done good does not make a fuss of it but goes on to the next good deed.`]},"ph-franklin-1":{e:`original-en`,t:[`TEMPERANCE. Eat not to dullness; drink not to elevation.`]},"ph-daodejing-44":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Know what is enough and you will not be disgraced; know when to stop and you will not be in danger. Then you can long endure.`]},"ph-epicurus-68":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Nothing is enough for one to whom enough seems too little.`]},"ph-meditations-4-24":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`It is said that if you want peace of mind, you should do few things. Better to say: do only what is necessary. Most of what we say and do is not necessary; cut it away and you will have more time and more ease, and less confusion. So at every turn ask yourself, "Is this necessary?"`]},"ph-seneca-2":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`It is not the person who has too little who is poor, but the one who craves more.`]},"ph-daodejing-64":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`A tree you can barely put your arms around grows from a tiny shoot; a tower of nine stories begins with a heap of earth; a journey of a thousand miles begins beneath your feet.`]},"ph-analects-1-4":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Master Zeng said, "Each day I examine myself on three points: In working for others, have I failed to do my best? In dealing with friends, have I failed to keep my word? Have I failed to practice what I was taught?"`]},"ph-brevitate-9":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The greatest waste of life is postponement. It takes each day away as it comes and steals the present by promising the future. The greatest obstacle to living is expectation, which hangs on tomorrow and loses today. Live now.`]},"ph-franklin-6":{e:`original-en`,t:[`INDUSTRY. Lose no time; be always employ'd in something useful; cut off all unnecessary actions.`]},"ph-seneca-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Claim yourself back. Gather and guard the time that until now was taken from you or slipped away. The time we lose most is the time we let pass without thought. Hold on to today, and you will depend less on tomorrow.`]},"ph-analects-13-17":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`When Zixia became governor of Jufu and asked about governing, the Master said, "Do not rush, and do not look to small gains. If you rush, you will not get there; if you look to small gains, great things will not be accomplished."`]},"ph-meditations-10-3":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Whatever happens is either something you are by nature able to bear or something you are not. If you can bear it, bear it without complaint. Even when it seems unbearable, remember that you can bear whatever your own mind judges bearable when it sees that doing so is for your good.`]},"ph-daodejing-63":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Plan for what is difficult while it is easy; do what is great while it is small. The difficult things of the world always arise from what is easy, and the great things of the world always arise from what is small.`]},"ph-mencius-13-29":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius said, "Setting out to do something is like digging a well. If you dig nine fathoms deep but stop before you reach the spring, it is still an abandoned well."`]},"ph-enchiridion-10":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Whatever happens, ask yourself what power you have to deal with it. Facing hardship, you will find endurance; facing insult, you will find patience. Once you are used to this, appearances will not carry you away.`]},"ph-meditations-4-3":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`People seek retreats for themselves in the country, by the sea, in the mountains. But you can retreat into yourself whenever you wish. There is no place more quiet or more free of trouble for a person to withdraw to than their own mind. So give yourself this retreat often, and renew yourself.`]},"ph-daodejing-16":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Reach the utmost emptiness; hold fast to stillness. The ten thousand things rise together, and I watch them return. Things flourish in abundance, yet each returns to its root. Returning to the root is called stillness.`]},"ph-analects-7-37":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "A noble person is calm and open-hearted; a petty person is always anxious and full of worry."`]},"ph-pensees-139":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`I have discovered that all human unhappiness comes from one thing: not knowing how to stay quietly in a room.`]},"ph-zhongyong-14":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Setting oneself right and asking nothing of others, one has no resentment: above, one does not resent Heaven; below, one does not blame others.`]},"ph-meditations-4-49":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Be like the headland on which the waves keep breaking: it stands firm, and around it the swell grows calm. Do not say, "How unlucky I am that this has happened," but rather, "How fortunate I am that, though this has happened, I remain untroubled."`]},"ph-analects-4-25":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Virtue is never alone; it is sure to have neighbors."`]},"ph-meditations-8-36":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Do not trouble yourself by picturing your whole life at once. Do not gather up in your mind all the hardships that may come, but ask about each present thing: "What here is unbearable?" What weighs on you is never the future or the past, only the present, and the present shrinks when you look at it on its own.`]},"ph-amicitia-22":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Friendship makes good fortune brighter, and lightens adversity by dividing it and sharing its weight.`]},"ph-meditations-7-64":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Whenever pain comes, remember that it is nothing shameful and cannot damage the mind that guides you. For most pain, let Epicurus help: pain is neither unbearable nor endless, so long as you remember its limits and do not add to it in imagination.`]},"ph-analects-9-23":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The young deserve our respect. How do we know that those who come after will not equal those of today?"`]},"ph-meditations-7-59":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Look within. Within is the spring of good, and it will always bubble up if you keep on digging.`]},"ph-daodejing-41":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The great square has no corners; the great vessel is completed late; the great sound is faintly heard; the great image has no form.`]},"ph-analects-9-19":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "It is like building a mound: if I stop when it is one basket of earth short, it is I who have stopped. It is like leveling the ground: if I have tipped out only one basket of earth but keep going, it is I who have gone forward."`]},"ph-zhongyong-20":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`If others can do it in one try, I will do it a hundred times; if others can do it in ten, I will do it a thousand. Whoever truly follows this way, though slow, will surely become clear-sighted; though weak, will surely become strong.`]},"ph-analects-1-2":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Master You said, "Few who are devoted to their parents and respectful to their elders like to defy those above them, and none who dislike defying those above them have ever liked to stir up disorder. The noble person works at the root; when the root is established, the way grows from it. Devotion to parents and respect for elders: are these not the root of humaneness?"`]},"ph-analects-1-3":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Clever words and a pleasing face: seldom is there humaneness in them."`]},"ph-analects-1-6":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The young should be devoted to their parents at home and respectful to elders abroad, careful and trustworthy, loving everyone broadly and keeping close to those who are humane. If they have strength to spare after this, let them study."`]},"ph-analects-1-7":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Zixia said, "Someone who values worthy people as much as beauty, serves their parents with all their strength, gives themselves fully in serving those above, and keeps their word with friends: even if they say they have not studied, I would surely call them learned."`]},"ph-analects-1-12":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Master You said, "In the practice of ritual, harmony is what is most precious."`]},"ph-analects-1-14":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The noble person does not seek a full stomach in eating or comfort in dwelling, is quick in work and careful in speech, and goes to those who have the way to be set right. Such a person can be said to love learning."`]},"ph-analects-1-15":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Zigong said, "To be poor without flattering, to be rich without arrogance: how is that?" The Master said, "Good, but not as good as being poor and joyful, rich and fond of propriety." Zigong said, "The Book of Songs says, 'As if cut, as if filed, as if carved, as if polished.' Is that what this means?" The Master said, "Ci, now I can talk about the Songs with you. Told what has gone before, you understand what is to come."`]},"ph-analects-1-16":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Do not worry that others do not know you; worry that you do not know others."`]},"ph-analects-2-4":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "At fifteen I set my heart on learning; at thirty I stood firm; at forty I was free from doubt; at fifty I understood the will of Heaven; at sixty my ear was attuned; at seventy I could follow my heart's desire without overstepping what is right."`]},"ph-analects-2-7":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Ziyou asked about filial devotion. The Master said, "Nowadays filial devotion means being able to provide for one's parents. But even dogs and horses are provided for. Without respect, where is the difference?"`]},"ph-analects-2-10":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "See what a person does, observe why they do it, and examine what they are at ease with. How can a person stay hidden? How can a person stay hidden?"`]},"ph-analects-2-11":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Whoever reviews the old so as to understand the new may be a teacher."`]},"ph-analects-2-12":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The noble person is not a vessel made for a single use."`]},"ph-analects-2-13":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Zigong asked about the noble person. The Master said, "First put your words into practice, and then let your words follow."`]},"ph-analects-2-14":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The noble person is inclusive and does not form cliques; the petty person forms cliques and is not inclusive."`]},"ph-analects-2-18":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Zizhang wanted to learn how to obtain a salaried post. The Master said, "Hear much, set aside what is doubtful, and speak carefully of the rest, and you will make few mistakes. See much, set aside what is risky, and act carefully on the rest, and you will have few regrets. With few mistakes in speech and few regrets in action, the post will follow."`]},"ph-analects-2-22":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "I do not know what a person without trustworthiness can do. If a large cart has no yoke-pin and a small cart no collar-pin, how can they move?"`]},"ph-analects-3-4":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Lin Fang asked about the root of ritual. The Master said, "A great question! In ritual, it is better to be frugal than extravagant."`]},"ph-analects-4-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "It is beautiful to dwell among the humane. If you choose not to live among the humane, how can you be called wise?"`]},"ph-analects-4-2":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Those without humaneness cannot remain long in hardship, nor long in happiness. The humane are at ease in humaneness; the wise find it beneficial."`]},"ph-analects-4-5":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Wealth and rank are what people desire, but if they are not gained in the right way, do not keep them. Poverty and low station are what people dislike, but if they cannot be escaped in the right way, do not abandon your place."`]},"ph-analects-4-9":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "A scholar who has set their heart on the way but is ashamed of poor clothes and plain food is not worth discussing things with."`]},"ph-analects-4-12":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Act only with an eye to profit, and you will earn much resentment."`]},"ph-analects-4-14":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Do not worry about having no position; worry about having what it takes to fill one. Do not worry that no one knows you; strive to be worth knowing."`]},"ph-analects-4-15":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Shen, my way is threaded through by one thing." Master Zeng said, "Yes." When the Master went out, the disciples asked, "What did he mean?" Master Zeng said, "The Master's way is simply wholeheartedness and understanding of others."`]},"ph-analects-4-16":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The noble person understands what is right; the petty person understands what is profitable."`]},"ph-analects-4-17":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "When you see a worthy person, think of becoming their equal; when you see an unworthy person, look within and examine yourself."`]},"ph-meditations-1-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`From my grandfather Verus I learned good character and a temper free from anger.`]},"ph-meditations-1-3":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`From my mother I learned reverence and generosity, to refrain not only from doing wrong but even from thinking of it, and a simple way of life far removed from the habits of the rich.`]},"ph-meditations-1-7":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`From Rusticus I learned that my character needed correcting and training; to read carefully and not be satisfied with a surface understanding; and to be easily calmed and ready to reconcile with those who had offended or wronged me as soon as they wished to make peace.`]},"ph-meditations-1-8":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`From Apollonius I learned freedom of mind and unwavering firmness; to look to nothing else, even for a moment, but reason; and to remain always the same, in sharp pain and in long illness.`]},"ph-meditations-1-15":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`From Maximus I learned self-mastery and not to be swept away by anything; cheerfulness in every circumstance, illness included; a character in which gentleness and dignity are well blended; and to do the work in front of me without complaint.`]},"ph-meditations-1-16":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`From my father I learned gentleness, and steadfastness in decisions reached after careful thought; indifference to outward honors; love of work and perseverance in it; and readiness to listen to anyone who had something useful to offer for the common good.`]},"ph-meditations-1-17":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`I had good grandparents, good parents, a good sister, good teachers, good companions, relatives, and friends: nearly everything good. For this I am grateful.`]},"ph-meditations-3-4":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Do not waste what remains of your life wondering about others, unless it serves the common good: what someone is doing and why, what they are saying, thinking, or planning. All this keeps you from other work.`]},"ph-meditations-4-7":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Take away your judgment, and "I have been harmed" is taken away. Take away "I have been harmed," and the harm is taken away.`]},"ph-meditations-4-17":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`While you live, while it is still in your power, be good.`]},"ph-meditations-4-18":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`How much free time is gained by someone who does not look at what their neighbor says, does, or thinks, but only at what they themselves are doing, to make it just and pure.`]},"ph-meditations-5-5":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`People may not admire your quick wit. Fine. But there are many things for which you cannot say, "I have no talent for it": honesty, seriousness, endurance of hard work, freedom from the pull of pleasure, contentment with your lot, needing little, kindness, generosity. You can show all these right now.`]},"ph-meditations-5-9":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Do not be disgusted, discouraged, or ready to give up because you have not always managed to act on right principles. When you fall, come back again, and be glad if most of what you do is worthy of a human being.`]},"ph-meditations-5-16":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Your mind will take on the character of the thoughts you dwell on most, for the soul is dyed by its thoughts.`]},"ph-meditations-6-19":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`If something is hard for you, do not think it impossible for a human being. If it is possible and proper for a human being, think that it is within your reach too.`]},"ph-meditations-6-21":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`If someone can show me and convince me that I am not thinking or acting rightly, I will gladly change, for I seek the truth, and no one was ever harmed by the truth. The one harmed is the one who stays in their own error and ignorance.`]},"ph-meditations-6-30":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Keep yourself simple, good, pure, serious, unpretentious; a friend of justice, reverent, kind, affectionate, and steadfast in doing what is proper. Strive to remain the person philosophy wished to make you.`]},"ph-meditations-6-53":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Train yourself to pay close attention to what others say, and as far as possible, enter into the mind of the speaker.`]},"ph-meditations-7-8":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Do not let the future trouble you. If you must face it, you will meet it with the same reason you are now using for the present.`]},"ph-meditations-7-13":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`As the limbs are parts of one body, so rational beings, though separate, are made to work together. This will strike you more deeply if you often say to yourself, "I am a limb of the body of rational beings." Then doing good to people becomes a joy in itself.`]},"ph-meditations-7-26":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`When someone does you wrong, consider at once what they took to be good or bad that led them to do it. Once you see this, you will feel sorry for them rather than being surprised or angry.`]},"ph-meditations-7-27":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Do not dwell on what you lack as though you had it, but pick out the best of what you have and think how eagerly you would have sought it if you did not have it. Yet take care not to delight in it so much that you would be shaken if it were ever gone.`]},"ph-meditations-7-71":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`It is absurd not to escape your own faults, which is possible, while trying to escape the faults of others, which is impossible.`]},"ph-meditations-8-47":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`If you are distressed by anything outside you, it is not the thing itself that troubles you but your judgment about it, and it is in your power to wipe that judgment out right now.`]},"ph-meditations-8-59":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`People exist for one another. So teach them, or bear with them.`]},"ph-meditations-9-6":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`A present judgment that grasps things truly, a present action directed to the common good, a present disposition that gladly accepts whatever comes from outside: that is enough.`]},"ph-meditations-10-16":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`No longer argue about what a good person should be like. Be one.`]},"ph-analects-4-18":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "In serving your parents, point out their mistakes gently. If you see they will not follow, remain respectful and do not go against them; though it may be wearisome, do not resent it."`]},"ph-analects-4-22":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "People of old did not speak lightly, for they were ashamed if their deeds fell short of their words."`]},"ph-analects-4-23":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Those who hold themselves in check seldom go wrong."`]},"ph-analects-4-24":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The noble person wishes to be slow in speech and quick in action."`]},"ph-analects-5-12":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Zigong said, "What I do not want others to do to me, I wish not to do to others." The Master said, "Ci, that is beyond what you have yet reached."`]},"ph-analects-5-15":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Zigong asked, "Why was Kong Wenzi given the name 'Wen'?" The Master said, "He was quick and loved learning, and was not ashamed to ask those below him. That is why he was called 'Wen.'"`]},"ph-analects-5-16":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said of Zichan, "He had four of the ways of the noble person: he conducted himself with courtesy, served those above with respect, cared for the people with kindness, and employed the people with fairness."`]},"ph-analects-5-20":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Ji Wenzi thought three times before acting. When the Master heard of it, he said, "Twice is enough."`]},"ph-analects-5-23":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Boyi and Shuqi did not hold on to old wrongs, and so there was little resentment."`]},"ph-analects-5-26":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Yan Yuan and Zilu were with the Master, who said, "Why not each tell me what you wish for?" Zilu said, "I would like to share my carriage, horses, and light furs with my friends, and not mind if they wear them out." Yan Yuan said, "I would like not to boast of my good deeds and not to push hard work onto others." Zilu said, "We would like to hear what you wish for, Master." The Master said, "To bring ease to the old, to be trusted by friends, and to embrace the young with care."`]},"ph-analects-5-27":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "It is hopeless! I have yet to meet anyone who can see their own faults and take themselves to task inwardly."`]},"ph-analects-5-28":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "In a hamlet of ten households there is sure to be someone as loyal and trustworthy as I am, but not someone who loves learning as I do."`]},"ph-analects-6-11":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "How worthy Hui is! A bamboo bowl of rice, a gourd of water, living in a shabby lane: others could not bear the hardship, yet Hui never loses his joy. How worthy Hui is!"`]},"ph-analects-6-12":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Ran Qiu said, "It is not that I do not delight in your way, Master; my strength is not enough." The Master said, "Those whose strength is not enough give up halfway. You are drawing a line for yourself before you start."`]},"ph-analects-6-18":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "When substance outweighs refinement, one is rough; when refinement outweighs substance, one is showy. Only when refinement and substance are well balanced is one a noble person."`]},"ph-analects-6-20":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Knowing it is not as good as loving it; loving it is not as good as delighting in it."`]},"ph-analects-6-23":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The wise delight in water; the humane delight in mountains. The wise are active; the humane are still. The wise are joyful; the humane live long."`]},"ph-analects-6-27":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "A noble person who studies widely in culture and keeps themselves in check with propriety will not stray from the path."`]},"ph-analects-6-29":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "How supreme is the virtue of the balanced mean! Yet it has long been rare among people."`]},"ph-analects-7-2":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "To take things in silently, to learn without tiring, to teach others without growing weary: which of these can I claim?"`]},"ph-analects-7-3":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Failing to cultivate virtue, failing to practice what I have learned, hearing what is right and not moving toward it, not being able to change what is not good: these are my worries."`]},"ph-analects-7-6":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Set your heart on the way, hold fast to virtue, rely on humaneness, and take your ease in the arts."`]},"ph-analects-7-8":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "I do not open the way for those who are not eager to learn, nor help along those who are not struggling to put things into words. If I hold up one corner and they cannot come back with the other three, I do not go over it again."`]},"ph-analects-7-16":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Eating coarse rice and drinking water, with my bent arm for a pillow: there is joy in this too. Wealth and rank gained unjustly are to me like floating clouds."`]},"ph-analects-7-19":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Duke of She asked Zilu about Confucius, and Zilu did not answer. The Master said, "Why did you not say: he is the kind of person who forgets to eat when absorbed, forgets his worries in his joy, and does not notice old age coming on?"`]},"ph-analects-7-20":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "I was not born with knowledge. I love the ancient ways and seek them diligently."`]},"ph-analects-7-25":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master taught four things: culture, conduct, loyalty, and trustworthiness.`]},"ph-meditations-9-42":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`When someone's shamelessness offends you, ask yourself at once: "Is it possible for the world to have no shameless people?" It is not. So do not demand the impossible. This person is simply one of those who must exist in the world.`]},"ph-meditations-11-18":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Gentleness that is genuine and unforced cannot be defeated. What can even the most violent person do to you if you stay kind to the end, gently set them right when the chance comes, and, at the very moment they try to harm you, calmly show them another way?`]},"ph-meditations-12-4":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`I have often wondered how it is that everyone loves themselves more than anyone else, yet values their own opinion of themselves less than the opinions of others.`]},"ph-meditations-4-8":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`What does not make a person worse does not make their life worse either; it cannot harm them from without or from within.`]},"ph-meditations-4-31":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Love the modest craft you have learned and find rest in it. Spend the rest of your life as one who has entrusted everything wholeheartedly, making yourself no one's master and no one's servant.`]},"ph-meditations-5-18":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Nothing happens to anyone that they are not formed by nature to bear.`]},"ph-meditations-5-20":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The mind turns what hinders its work into something that serves it, and makes an obstacle into a step forward. What stands in the way opens the way; what blocks the road becomes the road.`]},"ph-meditations-6-7":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Find your delight and rest in one thing only: moving from one act of service to others to the next.`]},"ph-meditations-6-11":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`When circumstances force you to be shaken, return to yourself quickly, and do not stay out of rhythm longer than you must. The more often you return, the better you will hold the harmony.`]},"ph-meditations-6-39":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Fit yourself to the circumstances allotted to you, and love the people whom fate has brought into your life. But love them truly.`]},"ph-meditations-7-5":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Is my understanding enough for this task or not? If it is, I use it as a tool given by nature. If not, I hand the work to someone who can do it better, or, if it is mine to do, I do what I can with the help of another. For whatever I do, alone or with others, should aim only at what is useful and fitting for the common good.`]},"ph-meditations-7-61":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The art of living is more like wrestling than dancing, for it must stand ready and firm against whatever comes at it suddenly.`]},"ph-meditations-7-73":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`When you have done a good deed and another has benefited, why look, like the foolish, for a third thing besides: a reputation for doing good, or something in return?`]},"ph-meditations-7-74":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`No one grows tired of receiving benefit, and acting according to nature is a benefit to yourself. So do not grow tired of benefiting others, by which you also benefit.`]},"ph-meditations-8-48":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`A mind free from passions is a citadel. A person has no stronger place to retreat to.`]},"ph-meditations-8-51":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Be not careless in what you do, nor muddled in what you say, nor wandering in what you think.`]},"ph-meditations-9-11":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`If someone does wrong, teach them a better way if you can. If you cannot, remember that kindness was given to you for exactly this.`]},"ph-meditations-11-9":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Just as those who stand in your way cannot turn you from right action, do not let them take away your goodwill toward them either. Keep both: firmness in judgment and action, and gentleness toward those who try to hinder or trouble you.`]},"ph-meditations-11-13":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Does someone look down on me? That is their concern. Mine is not to be found doing or saying anything that deserves contempt. Does someone hate me? That is their concern too. Mine is to be kind and good-natured toward everyone, ready to show even that person their mistake, not to reproach them or make a show of patience, but sincerely and generously.`]},"ph-meditations-3-12":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`If you do the work in front of you following right reason, seriously, vigorously, calmly, without letting anything else distract you, keeping the good within you pure; if you hold to this, wanting nothing and avoiding nothing, content with acting according to nature now and with truthfulness in what you say, you will live well. And no one can stop you.`]},"ph-meditations-6-22":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`I do my own part. Other things do not distract me.`]},"ph-meditations-8-8":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`You have no leisure to read. But you have leisure to check arrogance, leisure to rise above pleasure and pain, leisure to be indifferent to fame, and leisure not to be angry with the ungrateful, even to care for them.`]},"ph-meditations-7-65":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Take care not to feel toward the unkind as they feel toward others.`]},"ph-meditations-2-7":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Do the things that happen outside you distract you? Then give yourself the leisure to learn something new and good, and stop being whirled about.`]},"ph-meditations-12-19":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Realize at last that you have within you something better and more divine than the passions that pull you about like a puppet on strings.`]},"ph-meditations-10-35":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`A healthy eye should see everything that is visible and not say, "I want only green things." So too a healthy mind should be ready for whatever happens.`]},"ph-meditations-8-16":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Remember that changing your mind and following someone who corrects you is still an act of freedom, for it is your own action, carried out by your own judgment and understanding.`]},"ph-analects-7-28":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "There may be those who make things up without knowing, but I do not. I hear much, choose what is good and follow it; I see much and keep it in mind. That is the next best thing to knowing."`]},"ph-analects-7-30":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Is humaneness far away? If I truly want humaneness, it is here at once."`]},"ph-analects-7-34":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "How could I dare to claim to be a sage or humane? But it may be said of me that I strive without tiring and teach others without growing weary." Gongxi Hua said, "That is exactly what we disciples cannot learn."`]},"ph-analects-7-36":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Extravagance leads to arrogance, frugality to stiffness. Better stiff than arrogant."`]},"ph-analects-7-38":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master was gentle yet firm, dignified yet not harsh, respectful yet at ease.`]},"ph-analects-8-5":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Master Zeng said, "Able, yet asking those who are not able; knowing much, yet asking those who know little; having, yet as if having nothing; full, yet as if empty; wronged, yet not keeping score. Long ago I had a friend who lived like this."`]},"ph-analects-8-7":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Master Zeng said, "A scholar must be broad-minded and resolute, for the burden is heavy and the road is long. Humaneness is the burden they take on: is that not heavy?"`]},"ph-analects-8-17":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Study as if you could never catch up, and still be afraid of losing what you have."`]},"ph-analects-9-8":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Do I have knowledge? I do not. But if a plain person asks me something with an empty mind, I examine the question from both ends and do my utmost to answer."`]},"ph-analects-9-17":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Standing by a river, the Master said, "So it flows on, like this, never resting day or night!"`]},"ph-analects-9-24":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Can one fail to agree with words of correct admonition? But what matters is to change. Can one fail to be pleased with gentle words of advice? But what matters is to understand them. Pleased but not understanding, agreeing but not changing: there is nothing I can do for such a person."`]},"ph-analects-9-26":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "A great army may be robbed of its commander, but no one can rob even an ordinary person of their resolve."`]},"ph-analects-9-29":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The wise are not confused, the humane are not anxious, the brave are not afraid."`]},"ph-analects-11-16":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Zigong asked, "Who is better, Zizhang or Zixia?" The Master said, "Zizhang goes too far; Zixia does not go far enough." Zigong said, "Then is Zizhang better?" The Master said, "Going too far is the same as not going far enough."`]},"ph-analects-12-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Yan Yuan asked about humaneness. The Master said, "To master oneself and return to propriety is humaneness. If for one day you master yourself and return to propriety, the whole world will turn toward humaneness. Practicing humaneness depends on yourself; how could it depend on others?"`]},"ph-analects-12-4":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Sima Niu asked about the noble person. The Master said, "The noble person is without anxiety and without fear." Sima Niu said, "Without anxiety and without fear: is that all it takes?" The Master said, "If you look within and find nothing to be ashamed of, what is there to be anxious about or to fear?"`]},"ph-analects-12-16":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The noble person helps others bring their good qualities to completion and does not help their bad ones along. The petty person does the opposite."`]},"ph-analects-12-22":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Fan Chi asked about humaneness. The Master said, "Love people." He asked about wisdom. The Master said, "Know people."`]},"ph-analects-12-23":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Zigong asked about friendship. The Master said, "Advise your friend sincerely and guide them well, but if they will not listen, stop, so as not to bring humiliation on yourself."`]},"ph-analects-12-24":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Master Zeng said, "The noble person gathers friends through learning and is helped toward humaneness by friends."`]},"ph-analects-13-19":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Fan Chi asked about humaneness. The Master said, "Be respectful in private life, attentive in handling affairs, and wholehearted in dealing with others."`]},"ph-analects-13-23":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The noble person seeks harmony but not sameness; the petty person seeks sameness but not harmony."`]},"ph-analects-13-26":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The noble person is at ease without being arrogant; the petty person is arrogant without being at ease."`]},"ph-analects-13-27":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Firmness, resolve, simplicity, and slowness of speech are close to humaneness."`]},"ph-analects-14-10":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "To be poor without resentment is hard; to be rich without arrogance is easy."`]},"ph-analects-14-24":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "In the past, people learned to improve themselves; today, people learn to impress others."`]},"ph-meditations-6-26":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`If someone asks you how to spell the name "Antoninus," will you shout out each letter? If they get angry, will you get angry too? Will you not calmly spell it out letter by letter? So in life, remember that every task is made up of small steps. Keep to them without being disturbed, without answering anger with anger, and go through what is set before you step by step.`]},"ph-meditations-7-2":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Your principles cannot die unless the thoughts that keep them alive are put out, and it is up to you to rekindle those thoughts again and again. You can come back to life. See things again as you once saw them: that is what it is to live again.`]},"ph-meditations-7-28":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Withdraw into yourself. The reason that guides us is by nature content with itself when it acts justly and thereby finds calm.`]},"ph-meditations-7-63":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`It is said that no soul is willingly deprived of the truth. The same is true of justice, self-control, kindness, and the like. Keep this always in mind, and you will be gentler with everyone.`]},"ph-meditations-7-67":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Even if you have lost hope of becoming a great scholar or scientist, do not give up on becoming free, modest, sociable, and faithful to the right path. And always remember this: very little is needed to live a happy life.`]},"ph-meditations-8-22":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Give your full attention to what is in front of you, whether it is an action, a principle, or the meaning of words.`]},"ph-meditations-8-32":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`You must build your life one action at a time, and be content if each action does its part as far as it can. And no one can stop each action from doing its part.`]},"ph-meditations-8-40":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Take away your judgment about what seems painful, and you yourself stand completely safe.`]},"ph-meditations-10-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Will you ever, my soul, be good, simple, whole, and open, clearer than the body that surrounds you? Will you ever taste an affectionate and loving disposition? Will you ever be full and lacking nothing, longing for nothing?`]},"ph-meditations-10-30":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`When someone's fault offends you, turn at once to yourself and consider whether you have made a similar mistake. You will soon forget your anger.`]},"ph-meditations-10-32":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Let no one be able to say truthfully of you that you are not simple or not good. If anyone thinks so, let it be false. All of this is in your power.`]},"ph-meditations-11-4":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Have I done something for the common good? Then I have already been rewarded. Keep this always ready, and never stop.`]},"ph-meditations-4-26":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Has something happened to you? Very well. Everything that happens was woven into the whole as your share from the beginning. In short, life is brief. Make good use of the present with sound reasoning and justice. Stay clear-headed even when you rest.`]},"ph-meditations-5-3":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Consider yourself worthy of every word and action that is in keeping with nature, and do not be swayed by the criticism or talk of others that may follow. If it is good to do or say, do not think it beneath you.`]},"ph-meditations-5-22":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`What does not harm the community does not harm its members. Whenever you think you have been harmed, apply this rule: if the community is not harmed, neither am I.`]},"ph-meditations-5-31":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`How have you behaved so far toward your parents, siblings, spouse, children, teachers, those who raised you, friends, and neighbors? Can you say that until now you have done no wrong to any of them in word or deed?`]},"ph-meditations-6-20":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`In the gym, if someone scratches us with their nails or bumps our head, we do not get angry, hold a grudge, or suspect them of meaning harm. We just keep an eye on them, not with hostility or suspicion, but good-naturedly stepping aside. Let us do the same in the rest of life, and overlook much in those we contend with.`]},"ph-meditations-6-42":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`We are all working together toward one end, some knowingly and with understanding, others without knowing it. As it has been said, even those who sleep are workers who help bring about what happens in the world.`]},"ph-meditations-7-36":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`It is a royal thing to do good and be spoken ill of.`]},"ph-meditations-9-13":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Today I escaped from all my anxiety, or rather, I threw it out, for it was not outside me but within, in my own judgments.`]},"ph-meditations-9-27":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`When others blame you, hate you, or say such things about you, go into their souls and see what kind of people they are. You will see that you need not be troubled about what they think of you. Yet you must be kind to them, for by nature they are your friends.`]},"ph-meditations-12-17":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`If it is not right, do not do it; if it is not true, do not say it.`]},"ph-meditations-8-49":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Do not tell yourself more than your first impressions report. You are told that someone is speaking ill of you. That is what you were told; you were not told that you have been harmed. Stay with first impressions and add nothing from within, and nothing happens to you.`]},"ph-meditations-4-12":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Always keep two things ready: first, to do only what reason directs for the good of people; second, to change your mind if someone sets you right and leads you away from a view. But the change must come from being persuaded that it is just or for the common good, not because it is pleasant or brings you a good name.`]},"ph-meditations-7-58":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Whatever happens, keep before your eyes those to whom the same thing happened, and how they were distressed, found it strange, and complained. Why would you want to be like them? Instead, put all your attention into how to make good use of it. Then it will become material for you to work with.`]},"ph-meditations-8-26":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`A person's joy lies in doing what is proper to a human being: goodwill toward one's own kind, rising above the pull of the senses, sorting out plausible impressions, and contemplating the order of nature.`]},"ph-meditations-3-5":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Do not act unwillingly, or without regard for the common good, or without due consideration. Do not dress up your thoughts in fine words, or talk too much, or take on too much. Be cheerful, and do not depend on help from outside or on the calm that others give you. Stand upright on your own, not held upright by others.`]},"ph-meditations-8-50":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Is the cucumber bitter? Throw it away. Are there brambles in the path? Step around them. That is enough. Do not add, "Why do such things exist in the world?"`]},"ph-analects-14-27":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The noble person is ashamed when their words go beyond their deeds."`]},"ph-analects-14-30":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Do not worry that others do not know you; worry about what you are not yet able to do."`]},"ph-analects-14-34":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Someone said, "What about repaying resentment with kindness?" The Master said, "Then with what would you repay kindness? Repay resentment with fairness, and kindness with kindness."`]},"ph-analects-14-35":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "No one understands me!" Zigong said, "Why do you say no one understands you?" The Master said, "I do not resent Heaven, nor blame people. I learn from what is humble and reach toward what is lofty. If anything understands me, it is Heaven."`]},"ph-analects-15-10":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Zigong asked how to practice humaneness. The Master said, "A craftsman who wants to do good work must first sharpen their tools. Wherever you live, serve the worthiest of its officials and befriend the most humane of its scholars."`]},"ph-analects-15-12":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Those who do not think far ahead will surely have troubles close at hand."`]},"ph-analects-15-18":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The noble person takes what is right as their substance, carries it out with propriety, expresses it with modesty, and brings it to completion with trustworthiness. Such is a noble person!"`]},"ph-analects-15-19":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The noble person is troubled by their own lack of ability, not by others' failure to recognize them."`]},"ph-analects-15-21":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The noble person looks for the cause in themselves; the petty person looks for it in others."`]},"ph-analects-15-22":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The noble person is dignified but does not quarrel, sociable but does not take sides."`]},"ph-analects-15-23":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The noble person does not promote someone because of their words, nor reject good words because of who said them."`]},"ph-analects-15-27":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "Clever words disturb virtue. Lack of patience in small things disturbs great plans."`]},"ph-analects-15-28":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "When everyone dislikes someone, you must look into it; when everyone likes someone, you must look into it."`]},"ph-analects-15-29":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "It is people who can broaden the way, not the way that broadens people."`]},"ph-analects-15-30":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "To make a mistake and not correct it: that is the real mistake."`]},"ph-analects-15-31":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "I once spent a whole day without eating and a whole night without sleeping, just thinking. It did no good. It is better to learn."`]},"ph-analects-15-36":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "When it comes to acting with humaneness, do not yield even to your teacher."`]},"ph-analects-16-4":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "There are three kinds of friends who help you and three who harm you. Friends who are upright, sincere, and well-informed help you. Friends who are showy, fawning, and smooth-tongued harm you."`]},"ph-analects-16-5":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "There are three kinds of delight that help you and three that harm you. To delight in living with the measure of ritual and music, to delight in speaking of others' good points, and to delight in having many worthy friends: these help you. To delight in arrogance, in idle wandering, and in feasting: these harm you."`]},"ph-analects-16-10":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "The noble person has nine things to think about: in seeing, to see clearly; in hearing, to hear well; in expression, to be gentle; in manner, to be respectful; in speech, to be sincere; in work, to be careful; in doubt, to ask; in anger, to consider the trouble it may bring; and when there is gain in sight, to consider whether it is right."`]},"ph-analects-17-2":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The Master said, "By nature people are close to one another; by practice they grow far apart."`]},"ph-analects-17-6":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Zizhang asked Confucius about humaneness. Confucius said, "To be able to practice five things everywhere is humaneness." Asked what they were, he said, "Courtesy, generosity, trustworthiness, diligence, and kindness. With courtesy you will not be slighted; with generosity you will win people over; with trustworthiness people will rely on you; with diligence you will achieve results; with kindness you will be able to lead others."`]},"ph-analects-19-5":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Zixia said, "To learn each day what you did not know, and each month not to forget what you have mastered: that may be called loving learning."`]},"ph-analects-19-6":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Zixia said, "Study widely and hold firmly to your purpose; ask earnestly and reflect on what is close at hand. Humaneness lies in this."`]},"ph-analects-19-7":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Zixia said, "Artisans stay in their workshops to complete their work; the noble person learns in order to reach the way."`]},"ph-analects-19-21":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Zigong said, "The faults of the noble person are like eclipses of the sun and moon. When they err, everyone sees it; when they correct it, everyone looks up to them."`]},"ph-enchiridion-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Some things are up to us and some are not. Up to us are our judgments, our impulses, our desires and aversions: in short, whatever is our own doing. Not up to us are our body, our possessions, our reputation, our position: in short, whatever is not our own doing.`]},"ph-enchiridion-4":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Whenever you set out to do something, remind yourself what it involves. If you are going to the baths, picture what happens there: people splashing, jostling, shouting, taking things. You will go about it more calmly if you say, "I want to bathe, and also to keep my mind in harmony with nature." Do this in every undertaking.`]},"ph-enchiridion-6":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Do not be elated by any excellence that is not your own. If a horse were to boast, "I am beautiful," that could be borne. But when you boast, "I have a beautiful horse," know that you are boasting of something good that belongs to the horse. What, then, is yours? The use you make of your impressions.`]},"ph-enchiridion-8":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Do not wish for things to happen as you want them to; wish for them to happen as they do happen, and your life will flow smoothly.`]},"ph-enchiridion-9":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Illness is an obstacle to the body, but not to the will, unless the will itself chooses. Lameness is an obstacle to the leg, but not to the will. Say this to yourself about everything that happens, and you will find that it is an obstacle to something else, but not to you.`]},"ph-enchiridion-13":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`If you want to make progress, be content to seem foolish and ignorant about external things. Do not wish to seem to know anything. And if some people think you are someone special, distrust yourself.`]},"ph-enchiridion-15":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Behave in life as you would at a banquet. When a dish comes around to you, reach out and take a moderate share. If it passes by, do not hold it back. If it has not yet come, do not stretch out your desire for it, but wait until it reaches you.`]},"ph-enchiridion-17":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Remember that you are an actor in a play, short or long as the playwright chooses. Whether you are given the part of a poor person, a person with a disability, a leader, or an ordinary citizen, play it well. Your job is to play the part you are given well; choosing it belongs to another.`]},"ph-enchiridion-20":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Remember that it is not the person who insults or strikes you who wrongs you, but your judgment that they are wronging you. When someone provokes you, know that it is your own opinion that has provoked you. So above all, try not to be carried away by the impression at once. If you gain time and pause, you will master yourself more easily.`]},"ph-enchiridion-22":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`If you wish to follow the path of wisdom, be ready from the start to be laughed at. Many will mock you, saying, "Suddenly they have come back to us a philosopher!" But do not put on airs; hold fast to what seems best to you. If you stay the course, those who laughed at first will later come to respect you.`]},"ph-enchiridion-29":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`In every undertaking, consider what comes before it and what follows, and only then begin. Otherwise you will start eagerly, but when difficulties appear later, you will give up in embarrassment.`]},"ph-enchiridion-30":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Our duties are generally measured by our relationships. If it is a father, the duty is to take care of him, to give way to him in everything, to bear with him when he scolds. "But he is a bad father." Were you bound by nature to a good father? No, only to a father. "My brother wrongs me." Then keep your own place toward him. Do not look at what he does, but at what you must do for your mind to be in accord with nature.`]},"ph-enchiridion-33":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Set yourself a character and a manner that you will keep both alone and with others. For the most part, be silent, or say only what is necessary, and in few words. Do not laugh much, or at many things, or without restraint. Avoid swearing oaths altogether if you can; if not, as far as possible.`]},"ph-enchiridion-42":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`When someone treats you badly or speaks ill of you, remember that they do it because they think it is right. They cannot follow what seems right to you, only what seems right to them. If they judge wrongly, it is they who are harmed, for they are the ones deceived. Starting from this, you will be gentle with anyone who reviles you. Each time, say, "So it seemed to them."`]},"ph-enchiridion-43":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Everything has two handles: one by which it can be carried, and one by which it cannot. If your brother wrongs you, do not take hold of it by the handle "he wrongs me," for that handle cannot carry it. Take it instead by the other: "he is my brother, we were raised together." Then you will be able to carry it.`]},"ph-enchiridion-48":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`These are the signs of someone making progress: they criticize no one, blame no one, complain about no one. They do not present themselves as someone important or as knowing anything. When praised, they smile inwardly at the one who praises them; when reproached, they do not defend themselves.`]},"ph-enchiridion-51":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`How long will you put off demanding the best of yourself? You have already accepted the principles you should follow. What teacher are you still waiting for? You are no longer a child. From today, consider yourself worthy of living as a grown person who is making progress.`]},"ph-franklin-2":{e:`original-en`,t:[`SILENCE. Speak not but what may benefit others or yourself; avoid trifling conversation.`]},"ph-franklin-3":{e:`original-en`,t:[`ORDER. Let all your things have their places; let each part of your business have its time.`]},"ph-franklin-4":{e:`original-en`,t:[`RESOLUTION. Resolve to perform what you ought; perform without fail what you resolve.`]},"ph-franklin-5":{e:`original-en`,t:[`FRUGALITY. Make no expense but to do good to others or yourself; i.e., waste nothing.`]},"ph-franklin-7":{e:`original-en`,t:[`SINCERITY. Use no hurtful deceit; think innocently and justly, and, if you speak, speak accordingly.`]},"ph-franklin-8":{e:`original-en`,t:[`JUSTICE. Wrong none by doing injuries, or omitting the benefits that are your duty.`]},"ph-franklin-9":{e:`original-en`,t:[`MODERATION. Avoid extreams; forbear resenting injuries so much as you think they deserve.`]},"ph-franklin-10":{e:`original-en`,t:[`CLEANLINESS. Tolerate no uncleanliness in body, cloaths, or habitation.`]},"ph-franklin-11":{e:`original-en`,t:[`TRANQUILLITY. Be not disturbed at trifles, or at accidents common or unavoidable.`]},"ph-mencius-3-6":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The heart of compassion is the sprout of humaneness; the heart of shame is the sprout of rightness; the heart of yielding is the sprout of propriety; the heart that knows right from wrong is the sprout of wisdom.`]},"ph-mencius-3-7":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`A humane person is like an archer. The archer straightens their stance before shooting. If the arrow misses, they do not resent the one who beat them, but turn back and look for the cause in themselves.`]},"ph-mencius-3-2":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius said, "I understand words, and I am good at nurturing my flood-like energy."`]},"ph-mencius-6-2":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Wealth and honor cannot corrupt them, poverty and low station cannot move them, power and force cannot bend them. Such a person is truly great.`]},"ph-mencius-7-4":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius said, "If you love others and they do not draw close, look back at your humaneness. If you guide others and they are not well guided, look back at your wisdom. If you treat others with courtesy and they do not respond, look back at your respect. Whenever you do not get the result you seek, turn back and look within. When you yourself are upright, the world will turn to you."`]},"ph-mencius-7-10":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius said, "There is no talking with those who harm themselves, and no working with those who abandon themselves. Humaneness is the peaceful home of a person; rightness is a person's proper path. To leave the peaceful home empty and not live in it, to abandon the proper path and not walk it: how sad!"`]},"ph-mencius-8-12":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius said, "A great person is one who has not lost the heart of a child."`]},"ph-mencius-8-28":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The humane love others; those with propriety respect others. Those who love others are always loved by others; those who respect others are always respected by others.`]},"ph-mencius-8-8":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius said, "Only when there are things a person will not do can they do something worthwhile."`]},"ph-mencius-8-14":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius said, "The noble person goes deeply into the way because they want to find it for themselves. When you find it for yourself, you dwell in it at ease; dwelling at ease, you draw on it deeply; drawing deeply, you meet its source wherever you turn. That is why the noble person wants to find it for themselves."`]},"ph-mencius-11-11":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`When people lose their chickens or dogs, they know to go looking for them, but when they lose their heart, they do not know to look for it. The way of learning is nothing else than seeking the lost heart.`]},"ph-mencius-13-9":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`When people of old could carry out their aims, they spread their kindness to the people; when they could not, they cultivated themselves and showed it to the world. In hard times, make yourself good; when things go well, share the good with the whole world.`]},"ph-mencius-13-20":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius said, "The noble person has three joys, and ruling the world is not among them. That parents are both alive and siblings are well: that is the first joy. To look up without shame before Heaven and look down without shame before people: that is the second joy. To find the most gifted people in the world and teach them: that is the third joy."`]},"ph-mencius-13-4":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius said, "All things are complete within me. There is no greater joy than to look within and find sincerity. Strive to act with understanding for others, and there is no shorter way to humaneness."`]},"ph-mencius-13-6":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius said, "A person must not be without shame. If you are ashamed of having no shame, you will have nothing to be ashamed of."`]},"ph-mencius-13-24":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Flowing water does not move on until it has filled every hollow. So when the noble person sets their heart on the way, they do not reach further until each stage is complete.`]},"ph-mencius-14-35":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius said, "For nourishing the heart, nothing is better than having few desires. Someone with few desires may lose a little of themselves, but only a little; someone with many desires may keep a little of themselves, but only a little."`]},"ph-mencius-2-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius asked, "Enjoying music alone or enjoying it with others: which is more enjoyable?" The king answered, "It is not as good as enjoying it with others."`]},"ph-mencius-4-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius said, "Favorable timing from Heaven is not as good as advantages of terrain, and advantages of terrain are not as good as harmony among people."`]},"ph-mencius-7-23":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius said, "People's failing is that they like to be teachers of others."`]},"ph-mencius-13-15":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Mencius said, "What people can do without learning is their innate ability; what they know without deliberation is their innate knowledge. There is no small child who does not know to love their parents, and none who, growing up, does not know to respect their elder siblings."`]},"ph-zhongyong-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Nothing is more visible than what is hidden, nothing more evident than what is small. Therefore the noble person is careful when alone.`]},"ph-zhongyong-2":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Confucius said, "The noble person keeps to the balanced mean; the petty person goes against it. The noble person's mean lies in always finding what fits the moment; the petty person goes against it by having no restraint."`]},"ph-zhongyong-13":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Wholeheartedness and understanding of others are not far from the way. What you would not want done to yourself, do not do to others.`]},"ph-zhongyong-15":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The way of the noble person is like this: to travel far, you must start from what is near; to climb high, you must begin from what is low.`]},"ph-zhongyong-27":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The noble person honors their virtuous nature and follows the path of inquiry and learning, reaches what is broad and great while attending to what is fine and subtle, attains what is lofty and bright while keeping to the balanced mean. They review the old to understand the new, and with sincere warmth they honor propriety.`]},"ph-zhongyong-33":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`One wears a plain robe over embroidered silk because one dislikes making the pattern too conspicuous. So the way of the noble person is quiet yet grows brighter each day; the way of the petty person is flashy yet fades each day.`]},"ph-seneca-3":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Think long about whether to make someone your friend. But once you have decided, welcome them with your whole heart and speak to them as openly as you would to yourself.`]},"ph-seneca-5":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`You will stop fearing when you stop hoping. Fear and hope are chained together and walk side by side. Both arise when the mind is caught up in a future that has not yet come.`]},"ph-seneca-7":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Spend time with those who will make you better, and welcome those whom you can make better. This works both ways, for people learn while they teach.`]},"ph-seneca-9":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`I will show you a love charm that needs no potions, no herbs, no spells: if you wish to be loved, love.`]},"ph-seneca-12":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Let us cherish and love old age, for it is full of pleasure if you know how to use it. Fruit tastes best when it is fully ripe. Treat each day as a complete life in itself.`]},"ph-seneca-16":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`If you live according to nature, you will never be poor; if you live according to others' opinions, you will never be rich. Nature asks for little; opinion asks for no end.`]},"ph-seneca-18":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Set aside a few days in which you make do with the plainest food and rough clothing, and ask yourself, "Is this what I used to fear?" It is in times of ease that the mind should prepare for difficulty.`]},"ph-seneca-23":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Above all, learn how to feel joy. True joy is not a matter of laughter and chatter; it is a serious thing. It does not come from outside but wells up from within. Look to the good that is within you; that is truly your own.`]},"ph-seneca-28":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`You are surprised that all your travels and changes of scene have not shaken off your heavy heart. What you need is a change of mind, not a change of sky, for wherever you go, you take yourself with you.`]},"ph-seneca-40":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Thank you for writing to me so often, for in this way you visit me in the only way you can. A letter from an absent friend carries real traces, real evidence of them. To recognize a friend's handwriting is the next sweetest thing to seeing them face to face.`]},"ph-seneca-48":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`No one can live happily who looks only to themselves and turns everything to their own advantage. You must live for others if you wish to live for yourself.`]},"ph-seneca-50":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Why do we deceive ourselves? Our trouble is not outside us; it is within. That is why it is hard to recover. But if we wish to be healed, it is never too late, for the mind is supple and can be trained.`]},"ph-seneca-56":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`There is no true quiet except the quiet that reason has brought about. What does it matter how noisy it is outside, if all is calm within? If the mind is at peace with itself, the uproar outside cannot shake you.`]},"ph-seneca-71":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Our plans go astray because they have no aim. When someone does not know which harbor they are making for, no wind is the right wind.`]},"ph-seneca-76":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`You must keep learning as long as you do not know something; if we believe the proverb, as long as you live.`]},"ph-seneca-78":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Pain that is too great to bear does not last long, and pain that lasts long can be borne. And anything is light if opinion adds nothing to it. Do not make yourself suffer again now by recalling past pain.`]},"ph-seneca-98":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The mind that is anxious about the future is unhappy: it is unhappy before misfortune even arrives. So is the mind that frets that good things stay to the end. Such a mind never rests, and while it waits, it loses even what it could enjoy now.`]},"ph-seneca-106":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`We waste the strength of learning on needless subtleties. We learn not for life but for the classroom. But learning should be for life.`]},"ph-seneca-107":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Bear bravely what cannot be avoided. To follow, complaining, what cannot be changed is the mark of the worst kind of soldier. Fate leads those who are willing and drags those who resist.`]},"ph-seneca-123":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Nothing is heavy that the mind accepts willingly. What wears us out is not so much the task itself as doing it grudgingly.`]},"ph-brevitate-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`It is not that we have a short time to live, but that we waste a great deal of it. Life is long enough, and generous enough for the greatest things, if only it is well spent.`]},"ph-brevitate-3":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`People are tight-fisted in guarding their property, yet wildly wasteful with time, the one thing in which it is right to be careful.`]},"ph-brevitate-7":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Learning how to live takes a whole lifetime. So do not hurry; keep learning a little each day.`]},"ph-brevitate-14":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Only those who make time for wisdom are truly at leisure. They not only keep their own time well but add every past age to it. All the wisdom gathered over the years becomes theirs. The sages of old always welcome them, and send no one away empty-handed.`]},"ph-epicurus-17":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`It is not the young who are to be congratulated, but the old who have lived well. The young, in their prime, are tossed about by fortune; the old have come to anchor in old age as in a harbor, and keep safe in grateful memory the good things they once only hoped for.`]},"ph-epicurus-35":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Do not spoil what you have by longing for what you do not have; remember that what you now have was once among the things you hoped for.`]},"ph-epicurus-34":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`It is not so much our friends' help that we need, as the confidence that we can count on their help.`]},"ph-daodejing-2":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The sage handles affairs without forcing and teaches without words. The sage lets all things arise without turning them away, gives life without possessing, acts without relying on it, and accomplishes without dwelling on the achievement. Because the sage does not dwell on it, it never leaves.`]},"ph-daodejing-5":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Is not the space between heaven and earth like a bellows? Empty yet never exhausted, the more it moves, the more comes out. Many words soon run dry; better to hold to the center.`]},"ph-daodejing-7":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Heaven is long-lasting and earth endures. They can last so long because they do not live for themselves; that is why they live on. So the sage puts themselves last and yet comes first, sets themselves aside and yet is preserved. Is it not because they have no selfishness that they can fulfill themselves?`]},"ph-daodejing-8":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The highest good is like water. Water benefits all things without contending, and settles in the low places people dislike. So it is close to the way. In dwelling, choose the ground well; in heart, be deep; in giving, be kind; in speech, be true. Because it does not contend, it is free from blame.`]},"ph-daodejing-9":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Holding a vessel to the brim is not as good as stopping in time. A blade sharpened too fine will not keep its edge for long. A house full of gold and jade cannot be guarded; better to stop. Wealth and honor joined to arrogance bring their own downfall. To withdraw once the work is done: that is the way of heaven.`]},"ph-daodejing-11":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Thirty spokes share one hub, but it is the empty center that makes the cart useful. Clay is shaped into a vessel, but it is the empty space inside that makes it useful. Doors and windows are cut to make a room, but it is the empty space within that makes it useful. So what is there gives benefit; what is not there gives use.`]},"ph-daodejing-12":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The five colors blind the eye, the five tones deafen the ear, the five flavors dull the palate. Racing and hunting madden the mind; rare goods lead conduct astray. So the sage cares for the belly, not the eyes, letting go of that and taking this.`]},"ph-daodejing-15":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Who can let muddy water be still so that it slowly clears? Who can stir what is at rest so that it slowly comes to life? Those who keep to this way do not seek to be full.`]},"ph-daodejing-17":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`With the best leaders, people barely know they exist. Next come those whom people love and praise, then those they fear, then those they despise. Where trust is lacking, there is distrust. Unhurried and sparing with words, the best leaders finish their work, and the people all say, "It happened naturally, by itself."`]},"ph-daodejing-23":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`To speak little is natural. A whirlwind does not last the whole morning; a downpour does not last the whole day. Who makes them so? Heaven and earth. If even heaven and earth cannot keep such things going for long, how much less can people?`]},"ph-daodejing-24":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Those who stand on tiptoe cannot stand for long; those who take huge strides cannot walk far. Those who show themselves off are not seen clearly; those who insist they are right do not shine; those who boast have no merit; those who exalt themselves do not endure.`]},"ph-daodejing-26":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The heavy is the root of the light; stillness is the master of restlessness. So the sage travels all day without leaving the baggage cart, and though there are splendid sights, stays at ease and unattached.`]},"ph-daodejing-27":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The sage is always good at helping people, so no one is cast aside; always good at saving things, so nothing is thrown away. This is called carrying on the light. So the good person is the teacher of the one who is not good, and the one who is not good is the material for the good person's work.`]},"ph-daodejing-28":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Know the strong, but keep to the gentle, and you become a stream for the world. Being a stream for the world, constant virtue never leaves you, and you return to being a child.`]},"ph-daodejing-34":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The sage can achieve greatness because they never consider themselves great. That is why they can achieve greatness.`]},"ph-daodejing-37":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Without craving, and in stillness, the world will settle of itself.`]},"ph-daodejing-40":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Returning is the movement of the way; gentleness is how the way works. All things in the world are born from being, and being is born from non-being.`]},"ph-daodejing-43":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The softest thing in the world rides over the hardest. What has no form enters where there is no gap. Few in the world attain the teaching without words and the benefit of not forcing.`]},"ph-daodejing-45":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Movement overcomes cold; stillness overcomes heat. Clarity and stillness set the world right.`]},"ph-daodejing-46":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`There is no greater misfortune than not knowing what is enough, no greater fault than always wanting to gain. So the contentment of knowing what is enough is lasting contentment.`]},"ph-daodejing-47":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Without going out the door, you can know the world; without looking out the window, you can see the way of heaven. The farther you go, the less you know.`]},"ph-daodejing-48":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`In learning, you add something every day; in the way, you let go of something every day. Letting go and letting go again, you arrive at not forcing. Not forcing, yet nothing is left undone.`]},"ph-daodejing-49":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The sage has no fixed mind of their own and takes the people's minds as their own. Those who are good, I treat with goodness; those who are not good, I also treat with goodness; and so goodness is gained. Those who are trustworthy, I trust; those who are not trustworthy, I also trust; and so trust is gained.`]},"ph-daodejing-52":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Seeing what is small is called clarity; keeping to gentleness is called strength.`]},"ph-daodejing-54":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`What is well planted cannot be uprooted; what is well embraced does not slip away. Cultivate it in yourself, and your virtue will be genuine; in your family, and it will be abundant; in your village, and it will last; in your country, and it will flourish; in the world, and it will be everywhere.`]},"ph-daodejing-55":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`One who holds virtue in abundance is like a newborn child.`]},"ph-daodejing-56":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Those who know do not speak; those who speak do not know. Blunt the sharp edges, untangle the knots, soften the glare, become one with the dust. This is called the deep oneness.`]},"ph-daodejing-58":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Good fortune leans on misfortune; misfortune hides within good fortune. Who knows where it will end?`]},"ph-daodejing-59":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`In caring for people and serving heaven, nothing is better than restraint. Restraint means preparing early; preparing early means building up virtue again and again. When virtue is built up again and again, there is nothing that cannot be overcome.`]},"ph-daodejing-60":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Governing a great country is like cooking a small fish.`]},"ph-daodejing-66":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Rivers and seas can be kings of a hundred valleys because they are good at staying low. That is why they can be kings of a hundred valleys.`]},"ph-daodejing-67":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`I have three treasures that I hold and keep. The first is compassion, the second is frugality, the third is not daring to put myself ahead of others. With compassion one can be brave; with frugality one can be generous; by not daring to put oneself ahead, one can become a leader of capable people.`]},"ph-daodejing-70":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`My words are very easy to understand and very easy to practice, yet no one in the world can understand them or practice them. The fewer who know me, the more precious I am. So the sage wears coarse clothing but carries jade within.`]},"ph-daodejing-71":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`To know yet think you do not know is the highest; not to know yet think you know is a sickness. Only by recognizing the sickness as sickness can you be free of it.`]},"ph-daodejing-77":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Is not the way of heaven like drawing a bow? What is high is lowered, what is low is raised; what is too much is reduced, what is too little is filled out. The way of heaven takes from what has too much and gives to what has too little.`]},"ph-daodejing-78":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Nothing in the world is softer and weaker than water, yet for wearing down the hard and strong, nothing surpasses it, for nothing can take its place. That the weak overcomes the strong and the soft overcomes the hard, everyone in the world knows, yet no one can put it into practice.`]},"ph-daodejing-79":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Even when a great grievance is settled, some resentment remains; how can that be called good? So the sage holds the left half of the tally but does not press others for payment. The way of heaven has no favorites; it is always with the good.`]},"ph-ethics-1-7":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`One swallow does not make a spring, nor does one fine day. In the same way, one day or a short time does not make a person blessed and happy.`]},"ph-ethics-1-10":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`When someone bears many great misfortunes with calm, not from insensitivity but from generosity and greatness of soul, nobility shines through even in these.`]},"ph-ethics-2-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`We become builders by building and musicians by playing. In the same way, we become just by doing just acts, self-controlled by acting with self-control, and brave by doing brave acts.`]},"ph-ethics-2-6":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Virtue is a mean between two faults, one of excess and one of deficiency. It is the finding and choosing of what is right in feelings and in actions.`]},"ph-ethics-2-9":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Anyone can get angry; that is easy. But to be angry with the right person, to the right degree, at the right time, for the right reason, and in the right way is not within everyone's power, nor is it easy.`]},"ph-ethics-3-5":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Virtue is in our power, and so is vice. For where it is in our power to act, it is also in our power not to act.`]},"ph-ethics-3-7":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The brave person fears and endures the right things, for the right purpose, in the right way, and at the right time. The brave person feels and acts as the situation deserves and as reason directs, and their aim is what is noble.`]},"ph-ethics-4-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The generous person gives for the sake of what is noble, and gives rightly: to the right people, in the right amounts, at the right time, and gladly, without regret.`]},"ph-ethics-8-1":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`No one would choose to live without friends, even if they had all other good things. In poverty and other misfortunes, too, people think of friends as their only refuge.`]},"ph-ethics-8-3":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The most complete friendship is between good people who are alike in virtue. They wish each other well because each is good. Such friendships are rare, but they last as long as the friends remain good.`]},"ph-ethics-8-8":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Friendship seems to lie more in loving than in being loved. Mothers show this, who love their children without seeking to be loved in return.`]},"ph-ethics-9-4":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The good person relates to a friend as to themselves, for a friend is another self.`]},"ph-ethics-9-9":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`It seems strange to say that the happy person has no need of friends. Human beings are made to live with others, so the happy person too needs company. Life is hard for one who is alone, but with others it is easier to keep up one's activity.`]},"ph-ethics-9-12":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The friendship of good people is good, and it grows as they spend time together. They seem to become better by being active together and correcting each other, for each takes on the qualities they admire in the other.`]},"ph-epicurus-23":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Every friendship is desirable in itself, even though it began from the help we give each other.`]},"ph-epicurus-27":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`In other pursuits the fruit comes only after the work is done, but in the love of wisdom, delight goes hand in hand with knowledge. The joy does not come after learning; it comes while learning.`]},"ph-epicurus-41":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`We must laugh and love wisdom at the same time, while managing our household and seeing to our other affairs, and never cease speaking the words of true wisdom.`]},"ph-epicurus-52":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Friendship dances around the world, calling on us all to wake up and celebrate the happy life.`]},"ph-epicurus-66":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Let us share our friends' sorrows, not by lamenting, but by caring for them thoughtfully.`]},"ph-amicitia-23":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Friendship casts a bright hope over the future and keeps the spirit from weakening or falling. Whoever looks at a true friend sees, as it were, a reflection of themselves.`]},"ph-amicitia-44":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Let this be the first law of friendship: ask friends only for what is honorable, and do for friends only what is honorable. Do not wait to be asked; be ready, without hesitation, and have the courage to give honest advice.`]},"ph-amicitia-47":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Those who take friendship out of life seem to take the sun out of the world, for we have received nothing better or more delightful than friendship.`]},"ph-amicitia-80":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Whoever looks on a true friend looks on a second self. Everyone loves themselves, not to gain some reward for that love, but because each is dear to themselves. Unless this same feeling is carried over into friendship, a true friend will never be found.`]},"ph-amicitia-88":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Nature loves nothing solitary and always reaches for some support, and that support is sweetest when it is found in the dearest friend.`]},"ph-amicitia-104":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Since friendship cannot exist without virtue, value virtue highly; and, apart from virtue, consider that nothing is more excellent than friendship.`]},"ph-pensees-277":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The heart has its reasons, which reason does not know.`]},"ph-pensees-172":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`We never keep to the present. We look ahead to the future as if it came too slowly, as though to hurry it on, and we call back the past as if to stop it, since it slips away too fast. So we never live, but only hope to live.`]},"ph-pensees-397":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The greatness of human beings lies in knowing that they are frail. A tree does not know that it is frail.`]},"ph-pensees-365":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Thought makes the greatness of human beings.`]},"ph-montaigne-1-14":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`The taste of good and evil depends in large part on our opinion of them. What torments people is not things themselves but their opinions about things.`]},"ph-montaigne-1-26":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`A teacher should be chosen for having a well-made head rather than a well-filled one. Do not have the student merely memorize and repeat; let them digest what they learn and make it their own.`]},"ph-montaigne-1-28":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`If pressed to say why I loved him, I feel I can only answer: "Because it was he, because it was I."`]},"ph-montaigne-1-39":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`We must reserve a little back room all our own, entirely free, in which to establish our true liberty and our principal retreat and solitude.`]},"ph-montaigne-3-2":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`Each person bears the entire form of the human condition.`]},"ph-montaigne-3-13":{e:`app-en-secular`,t:[`We are great fools. "I have done nothing today," we say. What? Have you not lived? That is not only the most basic but the most illustrious of your occupations.`]}};export{e as default};